abdce4ac-f490-461a-b65b-bb746f608ecd	60186569-a432-4375-9bee-485c69f086d5	4c073a7a-7864-4341-85ce-869914b3c117	\N	COURT_LIGHTING_AND_LUX_METRIC_COMPLIANCE.pdf	GLOBAL ACADEMY LUMINANCE QUALITY & POWER EMISSION MANDATES ======================================================================== Document ID: LUX-2026-V4 | Section: Structural Lighting Enclosure Guidelines This handbook governs the operational safety protocols and mandatory lux emission boundaries for all physical floodlight hardware networks across the academy. SECTION 1: PLAYING FIELD ILLUMINATION THRESHOLDS To maintain a safe visual tracking environment for high-velocity ball metrics, all active court enclosures must conform to explicit real-time lux intensity metrics: 1. Minimum Playing Luminance: Standard match play requires a sustained baseline of no less than 500 Lux measured at the court surface layer baseline. 2. Elite Competition Envelope: Professional tournament blocks require a sustained minimum baseline of 750 Lux across the entire inner playing envelope boundary. 3. The Failure State: If a court's active telemetry indicates average lighting intensity has collapsed beneath the critical 400 Lux ceiling during scheduled hours, the court must be updated to 'Maintenance_Locked' instantly to mitigate athlete collision risks. SECTION 2: AUTOMATED EMERGENCY LOCKOUT TRIGGERS When an under-illumination safety breach is verified (average intensity < 400 Lux), the administrative matrix is authorized to trigger immediate scheduling overrides: 1. Floodlight State Overwrite: The agent must attempt to execute a system override command to toggle the 'FloodlightState' column to 'On' automatically. 2. Reservation Relocation: If forcing the power relay fails to restore the 500 Lux baseline due to ballast degradation, the agent must execute the booking-routing tool to flip player reservation slots to 'Cancelled' and queue affected player User IDs directly into the ShadowQueue system tracking matrices.	1	[-0.012062